{"step_index":3730,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T10:37:00Z","thinking":"**Investigating JS Errors**\n\nI'm currently focused on that JavaScript error. The code snippet with `gl.getAttribLocation` is the source, it seems. I'm trying to determine why it's failing to retrieve the attribute location. The `gl.vertexAttribPointer` and `gl.vertexAttribDivisor` calls hinge on this, so pinpointing the issue with `io` and `ic` is my priority.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Data Loading Issue**\n\nI've discovered the root cause of the WebGL error. The problem stems from asynchronous data loading.  `draw()` is called before the buffers for `aOffsetScale` and `aColor` are populated via `fetch('ast_nodes.json')`. This results in `gl.drawArraysInstanced` attempting to read from empty buffers, resulting in an \"out of bounds\" error and the render loop's demise. I need to ensure the data is loaded before the initial draw call.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/fix_webgl.py\nimport re\n\npath = '/Users/ajoxendine68/Desktop/SSOT_Agentic_Brain/ops/scripts/fullscreen_ui/sovereign_orbs.html'\nwith open(path, 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Make sure buffers are allocated immediately so WebGL doesn't crash on frame 1\nnew_init = \"\"\"// Load Real Codebase AST Nodes\nconst INST_COUNT = 100000;\nlet instData = new Float32Array(INST_COUNT * 4); // x, y, scale, phase\nlet colorData = new Float32Array(INST_COUNT * 3); // r, g, b\nconst instBuf = gl.createBuffer();\nconst colorBuf = gl.createBuffer();\n\n// Pre-allocate to pre